-- `lang!` and `pass!`: intermediate languages, and the passes between them.
--
--   lang! { pub Surface from Calc }
--
--   lang! {
--     pub Core extends Surface
--     Expr - Bin
--     Expr + Prim { op : String, args : [Expr] }
--   }
--
--   pass! {
--     pub lower : Surface -> Core
--     | Bin { lhs, op, rhs } -> Prim { op = op, args = [lhs, rhs] }
--   }
--
-- A language is written out as tables -- see "a language's code" below: a
-- node is a row, and a program is a handful of arrays, whatever its size.
--
-- Unless it extends a grammar's language, or another that does: then it is a
-- **tree** language, a program of it a lossless tree -- as the parser makes --
-- whose kinds are its own, `Grouped.NodeInfix`, and each production a pattern
-- over it, `GroupedInfix lhs op rhs`, binding its fields off a node's
-- children. What a pass between two tree languages writes is a tree of the
-- second, every byte of the source in it:
--
--   lang! {
--     pub Grouped extends Meadow
--     Operation - Ops
--     Operation + Infix { lhs : Operation, op : InfixOp, rhs : Operation }
--   }
--
-- A tree's node is its children, so a production a tree language adds holds
-- nodes -- of a sort, maybe one, or several -- or, `String`, a token; and a
-- field cannot be added to every production of a sort.
-- Every node also has a `meta` nobody writes, saying where it came from. One
-- read off a grammar also gets its conversion from the typed AST:
-- `surfaceFromCalc`.
--
-- A production is one of its sort's: an expression and a pattern may each
-- have a `Var`. What is written for one is named for its language, its sort
-- and itself -- `newCoreExprLam`, `corePatVarName`, the pattern `CoreExprLam`
-- -- or, for the one production of a sort of its own name, the name once:
-- `newCoreName`. In a pass none of that is written: a case is its
-- production's name, `| Lam { … }`, and a node is too, `Lam { … }`, which is
-- the one of the sort being written if it has one; where two sorts have a
-- production of the name, the sort says which -- `| Pat.Var { … }`.
--
-- A pass is the cases that change something. Everything else is written for
-- it: a function per sort, reading the source's tables and writing the
-- target's, the children of every node translated before its case sees them,
-- and a copy of each production the target language kept as it was. In a
-- case, the target's reading functions -- `metaCoreExpr`, `tyTypedExpr` --
-- read the nodes written so far, and `out` is the target being written, for
-- a function the case calls to write nodes into or read them from. A
-- production the target dropped or changed with no case is reported where the
-- pass is. In a case, a production of the target written with a
-- record -- `Prim { … }` -- is that language's, and gets the `meta` of the
-- node it replaces. A case's body is ordinary Meadow: it may call anything,
-- effects and all, and a `match` in it is parenthesised, since `|` starts the
-- next case. `here` is the `meta` of the node being rewritten: where a
-- diagnostic about it points (`Lingua.Diagnostic`).
--
-- A pass may carry a **context** down the tree -- what an elaboration needs,
-- an environment say:
--
--   pass! {
--     pub elaborate : Core -> Typed with env
--     | Lam { param, later body } -> (let a = fresh () in
--         let b = body ((param, a) :: env) in
--         Lam { param = param, body = b, ty = … })
--   }
--
-- Every function the pass is made of then takes `env` first, and hands it on
-- to the children it translates. A field a case names `later` is not
-- translated before the case runs: it is a function from a context to the
-- translated child, for the case to call with the context the child is in --
-- under a binder, the one with the binder in it. Without a context, a
-- `later` field is a function of `()`.
--
-- A case may also take a field **`raw`**: the child as the source has it, a
-- row of the source's tables, not translated at all -- for a case that has to
-- look at what a child is before it can say how to translate it. `src` is the
-- source's tables, to read it with; and each function the pass is made of,
-- `elaborateExpr src out env child`, translates it when the case is ready.
--
-- What a pass learns across the tree, rather than down it -- the binders it
-- met, say -- is a side table, `Lingua.Table`: an effect a case performs and
-- whoever runs the pass handles once, not a parameter every case passes on.
--
-- A pass may also go from a **grammar** -- the tree `syntax!` parses, read as
-- it is, with nothing copied out of it into tables:
--
--   pass! {
--     pub rename : Calc -> Core with scope
--     | Let { name, value } -> …
--   }
--
-- A case is a rule of the grammar and its fields are the rule's, bound by the
-- rule's pattern: a node child translated, a token its text. A field a case
-- takes `raw` is the child as the tree has it, a `Syntax` -- for a case that
-- looks at what was written rather than at what it becomes. Only the rules
-- the pass can come to from the root need cases: a grammar has many a pass
-- never translates on their own, read through a case above them.
--
-- And a pass from a grammar may go **to** one -- the same, say -- answering a
-- tree: every node no case is for kept as it was, and a case's `Rule { … }` a
-- node built of its fields, with the tokens the source had between them.
-- Nothing is copied that nothing changed under. In such a case `out` is the
-- tree being read, which `Lingua.Rewrite`'s functions take -- to ask a piece
-- its kind or its children, or to build a node of pieces a case has in hand.
-- What regroups a chain of operators by fixities known only once a program is
-- read is such a pass, and the rule it builds one that `syntax!` declares
-- `built`.

